% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1), the earliest notation with arithmetic (systems/absys/README.md, Notation; docs/decisions.md rule 23).
% IDIOM: a relation as name'=lambda x begin ... end: Foster and Elcock 1969 (map and item, examples/map.ab1, examples/triples.ab1);
% IDIOM: arithmetic as an assertion usable in every direction, a = b + 1: Foster and Elcock 1969 (tutorial ch. 8); Foster 1968, pass sets (docs/decisions.md rule 23);
% IDIOM: begin ... end groups the stage's relations into one conjunction, one assertion: Foster and Elcock 1969 (docs/decisions.md, Three notations).
% P16d2.n100, one instance of stages.json: its size and arrival order are structure, not constants (stages.json, about), so
% the relations are written out, in the instance's order, as P12's facts were (batch 1C). Unknowns x1 ... (and y1 ...),
% the problem's x_1 ... (y_1 ...).
% P16d2. % Two chains of n = 100 unknowns each, x1 ... x100 and y1 ... y100, which share no unknown, posed as one computation.
% Stage 1: every succ(x_i, x_{i+1}), then every succ(y_i, y_{i+1}), ascending, as one conjunction.
% Stage 2 (the problem file's stage 3; stages.json renumbers it): x_n = x_1. No stage constant, so no constants block.
% succ, P16 Vocabulary: succ(a, b) is the relation b = a + 1.
succ'=lambda a, b
    begin b = a + 1 end
